    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-e/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"An oil company is drilling for oil. It costs £5000 to drill for the first 10 metres into the ground. For the next 10 metres it costs an extra £1200 compared with the first 10 metres, thus it costs £6200 . Each successive 10 metres drilled into the ground costs an extra £1200 , compared with the cost of drilling the previous 10 metres. a) Find the cost of drilling 200 metres into the ground. ( ) 4 The company has a budget of £15,000,000. b) Determine the maximum depth, in metres, that can be reached on this budget. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:580.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q5-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:585.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) The successive ten-metre sections cost £5000, £6200, £7400, . . .. This arithmetic sequence has first term a = 5000 and common difference d = 1200. Its sum is n[2a + (n −1)d]/2. With n ten-metre sections, the arithmetic sum is Sn = n 2 [10000 + 1200(n −1)] [B1] [A1] = 600n2 + 4400n. For 200 m, n = 200/10 . = 20 Substitution gives S20 = (20/2)[2(5000) + 19(1200)] = 10[10000 + 22800], so the cost is £328,000 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) Working backwards from the budget gives 15,000,000 = n 2 [2(5000) + (n −1)1200] = n 2 [10000 + 1200n −1200] = n 2 [8800 + 1200n] = 4400n + 600n2. Rearrange, divide by 100, then by 2: 600n2 + 4400n −15,000,000 = 0, 6n2 + 44n −150000 = 0, 3n2 + 22n −75000 = 0. The quadratic formula gives √ n = (−22 ± 484 + 900000)/6. Discard the negative root. The positive root lies between 154 and 155. Check the two integers: S154 = 14,907,200 [B1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q5-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"and S155 = 15,097,000. [A1] The greatest whole number of sections within budget is 154, so the answer is 1540 m . [M1] [A1] Source clarification. The displayed answer assumes complete ten-metre sections, as in the primary handwritten answer: Charging proportionally within the next section would allow about 1544.89 m.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
