      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Start from (dy/dx) sec x = y2 −y. Moving differentials gives sec x dy = (y2 −y) dx, so dy/(y2 −y) = dx/ sec x = cos x dx. Factor y2 −y = y(y −1). Indeed 1/(y −1) −1/y = [y −(y −1)]/[y(y −1)]. Separate variables and use partial fractions:  y −1 −1 1  y −1 = sin x + C. dy = cos x dx, ln [M1] [A1] y y Integration on the left first gives ln |y −1| −ln |y|. Exponentiating gives |(y −1)/y| = esin x+C\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"= eCesin x. With a signed constant A = ±eC this becomes (y −1)/y = Aesin x. On the solution through y = 1 2, [M1] [A1] choose the branch 0 &lt; y &lt; 1. [M1] [A1] At x = 0, y = 1/2, ((1/2) −1)/(1/2) = Ae0, so A = −1. The initial condition gives C = 0. [M1] [A1] Therefore (1 −y)/y = esin x, [A1] hence 1 −1/y = −esin x, 1/y = 1 + esin x and y = 1/(1 + esin x) . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
