    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"S R A B 2.5 2.5 P Q 3 3 2θ C The figure above shows an isosceles triangle ABC where the angle ACB = 2 θ . A rectangle PQRS  is drawn inside ABC , so that S and R lie on AB , P lies on AC and Q lies on BC .\" style=\"--madas-width:565.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q9-question-2.svg\" alt=\"It is further given that AP = BQ = 2.5 and PC = QC = 3 . a) Show clearly that the perimeter of PQRS is θ + θ ( ) 3 5cos 12sin . b) Express 5cos θ + 12sin θ in the form R sin ( θ + α ) , R &gt; 0 , 0 &lt; α &lt; 90 ° . ( ) 3 ( ) c) Find the value of θ , given that the perimeter of PQRS is 10. 4\" style=\"--madas-width:584.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Bisect the vertex angle at C. [M1] Half the rectangle’s width is 3 sin θ, and its height is the projection of BQ onto the altitude, 2.5 cos θ. [A1] Thus PQ = 2MQ = 2(3 sin θ) = 6 sin θ and RQ = 2.5 cos θ. Doubling the sum of adjacent sides, P = 2(6 sin θ) + 2(2.5 cos θ). The perimeter is 12 sin θ + 5 cos θ . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) Expand R sin(θ + α) = R sin θ cos α + R cos θ sin α = (R cos α) sin θ + (R sin α) cos θ. Match coefficients to obtain R cos α = 12 [A1] and R sin α = 5. √ 122 + 52, so R = 13, [A1] with Squaring and adding gives R = α = arctan(5/12) ≈22.62◦. [A1] (c) Setting the perimeter equal to 10 gives 12 sin θ + 5 cos θ = 10, or 13 sin(θ + α) = 10. The equation is sin(θ + α) = 10/13. [M1] The principal angle is arcsin(10/13) = 50.28 . . .◦; its supplement is 129.72 . . .◦. Thus θ + α = 50.28 . . .◦+ 360◦n or 129.72 . . .◦+ 360◦n, n ∈Z.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q9-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Subtract α. The two branches in one turn give θ ≈27.66◦or 107.10◦. [A1] The triangle requires 0 &lt; θ &lt; 90◦, [A1] so θ ≈27.7◦. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
