    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Write f(x) = x2(x + 2) = x3 + 2x2 . Then f′(x) = 3x2 + 4x &gt; 0 [B1] [A1] for x &gt; 0, so the function is strictly increasing and hence invertible on its range, (0, ∞) . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:642.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-e/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) If f(x) = f−1(x), then f(f(x)) = x, [B1] and strict increase forces f(x) = x. For instance, f(x) &gt; x [A1] would give f(f(x)) &gt; f(x) &gt; x, and f(x) &lt; x [A1] gives the reverse: Thus x2(x + 2) = x. [A1] Divide by x &gt; 0 to get x2 + 2x −1 = 0. [A1] Completing the square gives √ √ x2 + 2x = 1, so (x + 1)2 −1 = 1, (x + 1)2 = 2, x + 1 = ± 2, and x = −1 ± 2. √ Its only positive solution is x = 2 −1 , a fixed point of both functions: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:672.0px\"></figure>",
